## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design systems that balance regulatory compliance (GDPR, CCPA) with operational resilience. Instant consent revocation is critical for multi-tenant SaaS platforms where data processing must stop immediately upon user request. The OTCA framework ensures observability, transformation, compliance, and auditing are built into the data path rather than bolted on afterward.

## Key Concepts

- **Edge policy evaluation and dynamic consent revocation**: Decoupling policy decisions from the control plane enables sub-second enforcement without round-trip latency.
- **Per-tenant residency enforcement**: Data sovereignty requirements mandate that telemetry remain within geographic boundaries based on tenant configuration.
- **On-the-fly redaction and stream pausing**: Selective field masking prevents data leakage while maintaining stream integrity for non-sensitive attributes.
- **Immutable data lakes and end-to-end audibility**: Append-only storage with cryptographic chaining ensures tamper-evident logs for compliance audits.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Edge Policy Engine - consent evaluation stub
function isForwardable(event, policyStore) {
  const policy = policyStore[event.tenantId];
  if (!policy) return false;
  if (policy.consentRevokedAt && policy.consentRevokedAt > event.timestamp) {
    return false;
  }
  return policy.active;
}

// Real-time redaction middleware
function redactPII(event, tenantPolicy) {
  const redacted = { ...event };
  for (const field of tenantPolicy.piiFields || []) {
    redacted[field] = '[REDACTED]';
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model consent revocation state to handle clock skew across distributed edge nodes?
- What consensus mechanism would you use to propagate revocation events across a globally distributed fleet?
- How would you handle in-flight data that was collected before revocation but not yet persisted?
- What metrics would you instrument to verify SLA compliance for the 2-second pause requirement?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical privacy controls in a real OTCA path, focusing on consent-driven data handling and per-tenant residency.

## Key Concepts
- Ingest pipelines with edge gateways and Kafka
- Privacy controls: redact/drop audio data, store digest only
- Per-tenant residency and retention windows

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(event) {
  if (!event.consent_opt_in) delete event.audio_payload;
  event.audio_digest = hash(event.audio_payload || '');
  delete event.audio_payload;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota enforcement across tenants?
- How would you monitor privacy policy violations in near real time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design offline-capable telemetry at the edge with guaranteed delivery and strict tenancy isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Edge buffering, per-tenant isolation, idempotent writes
- Out-of-order handling and replay-safe semantics
- Pluggable sinks and backpressure-aware flushing
- Testing offline durability and cross-tenant isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
class EdgeBuffer {
  constructor() { this.store = new Map(); }
  add(event) {
    const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.seq}`;
    this.store.set(key, event);
  }
  flush(toSink) { /* dedupe by (tenant_id, seq) and send in order */ }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement exactly-once semantics across edge and cloud sinks?
- How do you test failure modes like prolonged offline periods and partial flushes?
